    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d259">

                <pb facs="0475" n="1441" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p2/pg1441"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 724</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">601.6411/1–2651</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Deputy Assistant Secretary of State
                        for European Affairs (Bonbright) to the Secretary of State </hi><anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d259-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-01-26">January 26,
                        1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Proposed restrictions on the travel of Hungarian
                    Legation personnel in the US</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Discussion:</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The Hungarian Government has imposed restrictions, effective January 22, on the
                    travel of all foreign diplomatic personnel in Budapest, except by permit, beyond
                    an area bounded by a perimeter 35 kilometers (18 miles) from the center of
                        Budapest.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d259-f2"/> In accordance with the policy
                    recently approved by the Intelligence Advisory <pb facs="0476" n="1442"
                        xml:id="pg1442"/> Committee (Tab A<anchor n="3" xml:id="d259-f3"/>) we
                    propose to institute immediately equivalent restrictions on the movement of
                    Hungarian Legation personnel here. Similar counter restrictions have been in
                    effect against the Rumanian Legation since May 1950.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d259-f4"/> We have informed the British and French of our proposed
                    action and have received indications that they will probably take similar steps
                    against Hungarian official personnel in London and Paris.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d259-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Recommendation:</hi>
                </p>
                <p>That you approve the attached note (Tab B) to the Hungarian Legation and its
                    release to the press after delivery.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d259-f6"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Concurrences:</hi>
                </p>
                <p> EUR, L, CON, R, S/S–PR, and the Department of Defense concur in our proposed
                    action. Our Embassies in London, Paris, and Moscow and our Legation in Budapest
                    also concur.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d259-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d259-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by McKisson (EUR/EE) and apparently concurred in by Higgs and Campbell
                    (EUR/EE). Approved by Secretary Acheson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f2" n="2" xml:id="d259-f2-content">
                <p>The U.S. Legation was informed of the restrictions imposed upon the travel of all
                    foreign diplomatic personnel in Budapest in a Hungarian Foreign Ministry note of
                    January 19, the translated text of which was transmitted to the Department of
                    State in telegram 346, from Budapest, January 19. (601.6411/1–2051)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f3" n="3" xml:id="d259-f3-content">
                <p>Tab A was a copy of circular airgram of December 14, 1950, on “U.S. Policy Toward
                    Soviet and Satellite Representatives in the U.S.” For text, see <ref
                        target="frus1950v04#pg_60">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1950, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 60</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f4" n="4" xml:id="d259-f4-content">
                <p>For documentation on the counterrestrictions against the Romanian Legation, see
                        <ref target="frus1950v04#pg_1063">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, pp. 1063</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f5" n="5" xml:id="d259-f5-content">
                <p>Documentation on the exchanges with the British and the French is in file
                    601.6411.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f6" n="6" xml:id="d259-f6-content">
                <p>The note included as Tab B to this memorandum was delivered to the Hungarian
                    Minister in Washington on January 29. It informed the Minister that effective
                    immediately, members of the Hungarian diplomatic mission in the United States,
                    Hungarian employees of the mission, and dependents of those persons, were
                    prohibited from staying or traveling beyond an area bounded by a perimeter fixed
                    at 18 miles from the White House, without special permission. For text of the
                    note together with a brief explanatory statement by the Department of State,
                    both of which were released to the press on January 29, see Department of State
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, February 12, 1951, pp. 261–262.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
